{
  "0": "Across all four datasets, factorize the tied item embedding table as E=AB with rank max(1, floor(d/2)), retaining hidden width d and using the same effective E for input lookup and candidate scoring. Reduced embedding capacity may regularize sparse items and reduce parameters. Preserve positional representation, SASRec topology, history, readout, loss and optimizer. Save both factors and reproduce the parameterization in the predictor writer; retain one model per category.",
  "1": "Across all four datasets, reduce the current maximum context length to max(2, floor(0.75*L)), retaining the most recent interactions in chronological order and the current positional-indexing rule. Removing distant context may reduce noise and attention cost. Preserve embedding design, SASRec topology, readout, scoring, loss and optimizer. Apply identical truncation and positional handling in training and the predictor writer.",
  "2": "Across all four datasets, replace the current readout with 0.9 times the latest causal encoder state plus 0.1 times the masked mean of the latest five valid causal states. At each supervised position, pool only states at or before that position. This modest smoothing may stabilize sparse histories while retaining recent intent. Preserve history selection, embeddings, backbone, scoring, loss and optimizer; implement the same readout in the predictor writer within each single model.",
  "3": "Across all four datasets, halve each SASRec feed-forward intermediate width, bounded below by one, while preserving its input/output width, activation, dropout and residual connections. A narrower feed-forward bottleneck may reduce overfitting and computation without removing causal attention blocks. Keep attention depth and heads, embeddings, history, readout, scoring, loss and optimizer fixed. Store the width in each checkpoint and reproduce it in the predictor writer."
}
